WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.869 align:start position:0%
 
the mayor of mariopol in southern  ukraine says the city is on the brink of  a humanitarian catastrophe and must be  completely evacuated as soon as possible  mario paul has endured relentless  bombardment from russian forces in  recent weeks and mayor vadim buchenko  says nearly 5 000 people have died and  some 160 000 civilians remain in the  city without any power there is growing  concern that the city could soon fall  under russian control  meanwhile ukraine's defense ministry  says there is no evidence that russia is  planning to abandon its effort to  capture the capital kiev russian forces  are also stepping up their attacks in  eastern ukraine  [Music]  for just a few brief moments musicians  provided those inside this  metro station and bomb shelter with a  brief respite from the war  [Music]  music is a ray of light that helps our  souls survive  it brightens our lives stops us  panicking  and keeps away the darkness gloom and  bitterness of this war  we all need support like never before  if we classical musicians can provide  support to the people by playing our  instruments by doing what we can do best  we will do it anywhere  but across ukraine and with each day  that passes the death toll increases  this watch belonged to one of the war's  victims his son victor witnessed his  death  the russian soldiers just started firing  i looked at my dad  looked into the car  he was already choking on his own blood  i saw four direct hits to the windshield  but there is a faint glimmer of hope the  peace will return here in kiev the  president offered moscow a compromise on  the donbas this is peace talks between  russia and ukraine are set to resume on  tuesday in turkey  i understand it's impossible to make  russia give up the territory completely  that would lead to world war iii i  understand completely i'm aware of that  that's why i'm saying this is a  compromise but let's try to resolve the  question of the donbass the complex  question of the donbass  but in moscow there appears to be no  shift in russia's demands  we have to make sure that ukraine stops  assimilating itself with the west with  nato in the military sense  with threats to the russian federation  physical military threats to the russian  federation  death and destruction in ukraine  here in lev the funeral of a soldier  another day that a family has been  touched by the war  and for the latest we're joined now from  the ukrainian capital kiev by dw  correspondent matthias berlinger  matthias tell us what might a possible  compromise for a ceasefire look like  from a ukrainian perspective  well a compromise will be not where the  ukraine where ukraine sees the likely  outcome otherwise it wouldn't be a  compromise and i wouldn't uh think too  much about the outcome of any of these  talks right now for now i think this is  decided on the battlefield and the any  compromise will be uh reached when one  of the sides feels the need to back down  and this is not what we are seeing right  now we have seen some  um readiness to talk by russia that was  in the beginning saying that uh they  were  basically just setting their demands  zelensky said they were basically  setting an ultimatum um and we see some  things that zelensky has brought in this  neutrality issue but i also here i  wouldn't see too much of uh um  of of a position to give in here said he  would be ready to discuss this but that  also this had to be uh confirmed in a  referendum uh so he would not take the  responsibility for this hugely unpopular  uh com for com compromise that would be  so hugely unpopular i don't see that  these talks um  are serving in  at anything beyond keeping the channels  open trying to feel the positions i  don't think they're going towards any  result at the moment so it doesn't make  sense to talk about outcomes yet  give us a sense of the situation on the  ground in kiev where you are with this  new round of negotiations about to start  [Music]  yeah and kiev the situation is unchanged  what we're hearing is that around the  city in the northeast and in the  northwest and in the east uh  yesterday there was fierce fighting we  could hear it here in the city that the  shelling was more intense than otherwise  and it's quite calm now we've heard that  ukrainian forces have regained some  territory there um otherwise life is  going on here the cities has been  surrounded from uh  about 180 180 degrees so about half of  the ring of the city is closed  since almost since the beginning of the  war so there are not so many changes and  these negotiations of course people are  following after them but what's more  important for their life now is what's  going on in the fighting zones and  what's going on in the places where the  refugees come from uh this is what  people look at not so much of these  negotiations  dw correspondent matthias burlinger  reporting from kiev thank you  the german chancellor olaf charlotte's  has called for eu accession talks for  albania and north macedonia to begin as  soon as possible schultz made the  comments after a meeting with sweden's  prime minister magdalena anderson here  in berlin he said it was important that  the eu sped up talks with the two balkan  nations to prevent them from being  pulled into russia's sphere of influence  charlottes and anderson also discussed  the question of swedish neutrality as  the country considers  nato membership anderson said its eu  membership meant sweden was no longer a  neutral country his german chancellor  olaf  magdalena and myself agree that we want  to strengthen the eu and make it more  resilient as well  the turning point in history is  something that means we must achieve  real progress within the european union  thus we talked about the fact that the  time has come  to  have the albanian and macedonian  accession talks  begin  as soon as possible otherwise these  balkan states will be vulnerable for  russian influencing  and we need to  strengthen our capacity  in a number of different ways  dw spoke to swedish prime minister  magdalena anderson after that press  conference we asked her about schultz's  comments about eu accession talks with  albania north macedonia here's what she  had to say  well i mean countries should be able to  join the european union when they are  ready for it and i think it's important  that all countries can have a process  coming closer to a european membership  and from a swedish perspective we have  been supporting countries for instance  ukraine uh to help build their  institutions to become so they could  come closer to becoming members of the  european union attacking ukraine was not  only an attack on ukraine but also on  the european security order which means  that every country can make their  decision themselves so of course we then  we have the right to do the decision  that we think is right for our country  but of course when we do we do that we  have to  weigh all the different risks with the  different options that we have the  situation in around the baltic sea has  changed after russia's invasion of  ukraine we've had a deteriorating  security situation around the baltic for  a long time but this was really a shift  what we have done from a swedish  perspective during the last years is to  increase our defense spending and we  also follow germany to go up to two  percent of gdp but we also increased our  bilateral multilateral cooperation like  being a partner to nato with a very  close close cooperation to with  finland but also with the joint  expeditionary force with with other  countries and we will continue in this  direction but we also have a discussion  with the other parties in parliament to  analyze the situation and see what that  means for  how we will act in the future and in the  ukrainian city of la viv is our  correspondent amy and essev for us  tonight it's good to see you amiens so  we are in week five of this war what is  the situation there in western ukraine  well brent i'm actually speaking to you  from the basement of the building that  i'm staying in which serves as a bomb  shelter there's been um air raid sirens  going off uh this is the second time  today so it's the second time i've had  to go down to the bomb shelter um there  were strikes four  more than 48 hours ago  around  this city oil uh reserves  from russian missiles launched all the  way from crimea so it has people  startled here but there haven't been any  missile attacks um  for over 48 hours but there were reports  of attacks um just 150 kilometers north  of here you probably can hear kids  playing around here right now people are  trying to make the best of it because  this happens uh daily  it's not more than once a day that we  have to uh when the air raid sirens go  to go downstairs but  look uh a lot of this is  symbolic strikes from the russian  military because the real fighting is  happening the real suffering is  happening in eastern ukraine uh the  donbass um kharkiv mariupol and um even  a lot more in kiev than it is here in  western ukraine brand yeah you know i'm  hearing those children knowing that  they're sharing the same space with you  um in a bomb shelter and that we're  talking about war and peace it you know  it just speaks to the brutal reality  of  this conflict and what about  a possible  ceasefire we know that there is at least  one new idea that's being floated  from the ukrainian perspective what can  you tell us about that  ceasefire has  become more real because of these  comments from the ukrainian presidents  basically what he said is that  he's willing to talk he's willing to  give uh ukrainian neutrality so he has  hinted before that this would be on the  table but it sounds in his remarks that  this is the furthest he's gone and said  that a neutrality would be an option of  course he said he would have to put this  up to  a referendum in the country and just  speaking to the people here on the  ground i don't know how many people  would vote for anything that would give  russia any kind of uh advantage here or  give them any ground now the donbass is  a different question basically what uh  zalenski said was that instead of  demanding that all russian troops leave  the country he would reset to basically  february 23rd before the russian uh  complete the complete russian invasion  of this country  and then decide the question of the  donbass  after that  they went back to that being the only  disputed territory but then the russian  troops would be out of the rest of the  country uh  like uh in kiev for example  i mean let me just ask you before we run  out of time the ukrainian government we  understand investigating possible  torture of russian soldiers by its  troops what are the allegations  well this is an interesting case brian  it's also interesting to see how the  ukrainian government reacted there were  videos circulating yesterday mostly on  pro-russian accounts on social media i'm  showing gruesome videos i watch this  myself i don't recommend watching it it  is  it is truly torture if it is real um  showing videos of soldiers shooting  ukrainian soldiers shooting russians in  the leg  as soon as they got out of the truck uh  breaking their bones beating them into  almost so that they're unrecognizable  now the government first said that  this was fake videos that this was  russian disinformation but um then they  said that if it were true they're going  to investigate it they will punish  anybody who was involved if it turns out  to be true because they said ukrainian  army is a european army and they follow  international standards and this indeed  if it was true would be a crime and  needs to be punished  our correspondent ami and essev in the  western ukrainian city of levive tonight  ami and thank you  you

